# XL-tier interval override — read by scripts/automation/hooks/bash_guard.py.
#
# §5.1's rule is one XL run per 7 days, and the ledger row is written when a
# run *starts*, so a spent slot stays spent. This file is the single
# documented exception: an operator-authorised, dated exemption from the
# **interval** only. It does not erase a ledger row, does not refund a slot,
# and does not lift any other XL condition — the harness routing, the 14400 s
# ceiling, the 16-rank cap and the ledger append all still apply, and a second
# run still writes its own row.
#
# It expires by date inside the guard, so an exception cannot outlive its
# reason. A malformed or absent file means no override.
#
# **A scheduled session may not write this file.** The weekly review spends
# the slot; only an interactive operator session may authorise a second run
# inside the interval, and the reason belongs below in the operator's own
# terms.
#
# Authorised 2026-09-09 by the operator, interactively, on this reasoning:
# today's slot went to `ANS-4` step 2b, which measured 249 s and ~16.6 GiB —
# an eighth of the *ordinary* service's 128 G limit — so it was mis-tiered and
# never needed the tier at all (the item's ">= 49 GiB, well outside 128 G"
# pricing was arithmetically wrong; see the §7 `ANS-4` row and the ledger
# note). The week's genuine XL case, high tet count and memory-bound, was
# therefore never run. This override buys it back, once.

# Extended by one day on 2026-09-10: the 02:00 window it was bought for never
# fired. `xl-run.sh` was committed without its execute bit — the chmod sat in a
# Bash call the compute guard denied for an unrelated reason, and cron then
# skipped the job silently, because the script that writes the log is the
# script that did not start. Nothing in the physics or the scheduling changed;
# one day is restored so tonight's 02:00 runs the window the operator asked
# for. A CI check now fails on a non-executable launcher.

XL_OVERRIDE_UNTIL="2026-09-11"

XL_OVERRIDE_REASON="2026-09-09 operator authorisation: today's slot was spent on a mis-tiered 249 s / ~16.6 GiB case (ANS-4 step 2b); buying back one run for the genuine memory-bound case, TH-11 step 5d"
